\textbf{Introduction}
Clustering is a fundamental task in machine learning, yet it remains difficult due to the lack of labeled data. Recently, deep clustering has emerged as a powerful solution, leveraging deep neural networks to learn features and clustering algorithms to group them. Despite this, the two-stage nature of current methods often results in suboptimal performance. To address this, we propose a new deep clustering framework that unifies feature extraction and clustering within a single model. Our method learns features using a deep neural network while simultaneously optimizing them for clustering through a dedicated loss function. We validate the effectiveness of our approach on the CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 datasets, showing that it surpasses existing state-of-the-art deep clustering methods. Furthermore, we offer a theoretical analysis of our framework and explore its connections to self-supervised learning. Finally, we introduce a novel clustering loss function that merges the strengths of our method and self-supervised learning techniques.

