\section*{1. INTRODUCTION}
Sequential recommendation systems are designed to predict a user's next action by leveraging their extensive past interactions. These systems are widely applied in various domains, including e-commerce, social media, and online advertising. However, accurately modeling user preferences remains a significant challenge. Although recent research has explored various methods to capture temporal dependencies, few have addressed the issue of noisy or irrelevant information. To overcome this, we introduce a new attention mechanism that captures both temporal and semantic dependencies. Additionally, we develop a novel semantic similarity metric to improve the modeling of latent user preferences. Our experimental results show that this approach significantly outperforms state-of-the-art methods. The source code can be found at https://github.com/ChenYi1999/ST-Rec.

\section*{2. RELATED WORK}
